<Docs
  title="Quota ledger"
  description="The local record behind the quota-aware agent pool, its persisted ledger and availability states."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Slot persistence, the ledger, selection, release and pool controls are implemented">
    The pool slot record, the append-only quota ledger, the six availability states, durable
    priority selection, idle release and pool controls now exist. Settings → Agent pool reads the
    status snapshot and supports reordering, enable/disable, limit/reset corrections, cooldown
    clearing and status refreshes for existing slots. Task-start selection and the one-turn force
    override are still planned, so tasks do not choose a pool slot yet. This page records the agreed
    contract so the implementation, tests and user guide cannot drift.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
  <p>
    <Badge tone="warning" variant="subtle">partly implemented</Badge> The pool work is split into
    slot persistence, the quota ledger, priority selection, idle release, core controls, the pool UI
    and task-start wiring. Persistence, the ledger, selection, release, status, reordering,
    enable/disable, limit/reset correction and cooldown clearing are implemented and tested;
    Settings → Agent pool exposes all of them. Task-start wiring and the force-slot control are not
    checked off. The user-facing promise is on the
    <a href="/docs/users/agent-pool">agent pool</a> page; this page is for the implementation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="slot">The slot record</h2>
  <p>
    A slot is an installed agent plus one provider/model plus the credential or account it uses. A
    persisted slot needs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The user's priority order and the provider/model metadata.</li>
    <li>A local usage ledger, reset window, cooldown state and last-used timestamp.</li>
    <li>A reference to the credential or account. Never copy the credential into the slot, logs or database.</li>
    <li>An enabled/disabled state and the last error or health signal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ledger">The ledger</h2>
  <p>
    The ledger is local and append-only. Each observation records its source (catalog, provider or
    user), timestamp, used and limit values, unit, reset time, cooldown and error. A later
    observation, including a user correction, is the current one. The ledger is not a provider
    billing record and must not claim a limit it cannot observe.
  </p>
  <p>
    A quota response is evidence for a slot, not a global fact. The source and timestamp are
    preserved so a later reset or correction can be explained. If the provider reports no capacity,
    the observation leaves the usage or limit unknown; never translate that to free or unlimited.
  </p>

  <h2 id="availability">Availability</h2>
  <p>
    Before selection, a slot answers whether it can accept work at the current time. The derived
    states are available, exhausted, cooling down, disabled, unhealthy and unknown. A disabled slot
    wins first; an active cooldown, then a recorded error, then an expired reset window are checked
    before the used/limit comparison. Expired reset periods make a known limit available again;
    unknown usage or limits stay unknown. Exhausted and cooling-down slots are skipped with a visible reason,
    and unknown capacity is not treated as available.
  </p>

  <h2 id="selection">Selection</h2>
  <p>
    Selection sorts slots by the user's priority, then creation time and id, and returns the first
    slot whose derived availability is available. A disabled, exhausted, cooling-down, unhealthy or
    unknown slot is skipped with its state and reason, and selection continues to the next
    candidate; if no slot is eligible the outcome says so. An explicit task override is tried
    first; a missing or unavailable override is recorded and selection falls back to priority
    order.
  </p>
  <p>
    A selection is a durable reservation. The chosen slot, skipped candidates and reason are written
    with the request, and a partial unique index allows only one active reservation per slot, so a
    concurrent task gets the next slot instead of sharing one. A later request reuses its own active
    reservation only while the slot is still available; an expired or unavailable slot is released
    and selected again. Releasing a selection makes the slot available without deleting the audit
    row. The selection outcome names the chosen slot and every skipped slot, so a fallback is
    observable. A slot that is exhausted for one provider/model does not block an unrelated slot.
  </p>

  <h2 id="release">Idle release</h2>
  <p>
    Once a turn settles, the owner marks the reservation idle with the settle time. Only a settled
    reservation is eligible. The release call applies a configurable idle timeout (default 15
    minutes) and an active-request set; a running turn or pending approval stays reserved even if a
    stale idle marker exists. A reservation without an idle marker is kept with the reason
    <code>turn has not settled</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Releasing sets only the selection's release time. It keeps the audit row, the chosen slot and the
    reasons, and it never ends or clears the session record. The slot becomes selectable again, and
    a later selection for the same request clears its idle marker so active work is not released.
    The stored adapter session handle remains available for a resume; if the adapter cannot resume,
    the task says so and the next turn selects another slot. This slice exposes the core release API;
    the task runner that marks settled requests and supplies the active set is later work. The
    Settings pool panel manages existing slots but does not start tasks from the pool.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">Corrections and privacy</h2>
  <p>
    The core exposes a status snapshot joining each slot with its latest quota entry, derived
    availability and active request. Settings → Agent pool uses that snapshot to show
    provider/model, priority, availability, known limit/reset, current task, last error and last-used
    time; it never reads the stored credential reference. Reordering assigns the submitted order to
    priorities <code>0..n-1</code>; it requires every current slot exactly once and is idempotent.
    Enabling or disabling a slot also repeats safely, and a disabled slot is skipped by selection.
    Successful actions surface the store's reason, while failures and status-read errors stay on the
    page with a retry.
  </p>
  <p>
    A limit correction appends a user-source observation with the submitted limit, unit and reset
    time. It preserves the current usage, cooldown and error, mirrors the corrected values onto the
    slot, and refuses to run without advancing past the latest observation. Repeating the same
    correction returns the existing observation with <code>applied: false</code> and an explanatory
    reason. Clearing a cooldown appends a user observation with the cooldown removed while preserving
    usage, limit, reset and error; repeating it is also a no-op. A missing slot, an empty id, a unit
    without a limit, a blank unit or a timestamp that does not advance the ledger is rejected with a
    visible error.
  </p>
  <p>
    A forced slot still uses the selection request's one-turn override. That control is not wired
    into the UI yet. The pending work is to expose it, not to add another selection path.
  </p>
  <p>
    The catalog is advisory. A local ledger and the user's correction are authoritative for local
    selection; a stale catalog snapshot must not silently overwrite them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tests">Tests</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Implemented: ledger round-trips both stores, ordering, re-open durability, owner checks and rejected invalid input.</li>
    <li>Implemented: a deterministic clock covers reset periods, cooldowns, provider errors, clock changes, user corrections and every availability state.</li>
    <li>Implemented: priority selection and fallback against both stores, including every unavailable state, precedence, an expired reset period, overrides, reservations, concurrent requests, deterministic tie-breaking and re-open durability.</li>
    <li>Implemented: malformed selection rows and invalid requests or timestamps are rejected.</li>
    <li>Implemented: idle release against both stores, including the timeout boundary, repeat release, active and unsettled reservations, session resumability and slot reuse; a Turso re-open covers durability.</li>
    <li>Implemented: pool status, reordering and enable/disable against both stores, including latest quota/availability/active request, disabled selection fallback, repeat calls and rejected duplicate, missing or unknown orders.</li>
    <li>Implemented: the Settings pool UI loads the status snapshot, reorders slots, toggles enable/disable, surfaces outcome reasons and errors, and retries a failed status read.</li>
    <li>Implemented: limit/reset corrections and cooldown clearing against both stores, including preserved usage/error fields, repeat no-ops, concurrent identical corrections writing once, rejected stale observations and missing slots, plus the Settings form and its rejected-correction feedback.</li>
    <li>Pending: task-start wiring, the one-turn force control, cancellation and restart scheduling.</li>
    <li>Credential redaction in records, logs and diagnostics.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The core release and control APIs, corrections and the Settings pool UI are tested; task-start
    wiring, cancellation and the one-turn force control remain next-step items. See the
    <a href="/docs/contributors/test-fixtures">test fixtures</a> page for the fixture rules that
    apply when the work lands.
  </p>
</Docs>
